\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Gbaya-Mbodomo (ISO 639-3 gmm; Glottocode gbay1281; also Mbodomo, Bodomo, Mbadomo) is a Gbaya language of eastern Cameroon, also listed for the Central African Republic. Speaker estimates differ between sources (30,000 and 38,000). Documentation found is thin.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Mbodomo language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mbodomo_language}
\item SIL Cameroon archive entries, including a 1996 French-language lexicon by Virginia Boyd and a 2002 interlinearized folk tale: \url{https://www.silcam.org/resources/archives/32272} and \url{https://www.silcam.org/resources/archives/47623}
\item Huma-Num CRDO language entry (notes a grammar by Boyd, 1997): \url{https://cocoon.huma-num.fr/exist/crdo/lexvo.xql?code=gmm}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project, Gbaya, Mbadomo in Cameroon: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/19813}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network, Gbaya-Mbodomo: \url{https://www.globalrecordings.net/en/language/gmm}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is not known to be taught in schools. The Boyd lexicon, grammar and text would be starting points, but no curriculum-ready material was found.
\end{document}
